  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 1220 AM CDT Tue Jul 21 2026\n\nThe simple summer regime continues, with the strong midlevel \nhigh centered near Denver at midnight. The high is forecast to \nmove southeast directly over SW KS Tuesday, with very strong \nsubsidence under 597 dm heights and another very hot afternoon. \nNBM guidance has been persistently several degrees too cool for \nseveral days, and suspect this will continue today. Added about \n2-3 degrees to the NBM Tuesday afternoon at most locations. A \nweak backdoor cold front is expected to be near I-70 at sunrise,\nand slowly backdoor its way southwestward across SW KS during \nthe daylight hours. Elevated northeast to east winds are \nexpected behind the frontal passage this afternoon gusting\n25-30 mph. Despite this northeast wind shift and the complete \nabsence of downslope compression, temperatures will still easily\nsurpass 100 Tuesday afternoon, along and south of US 50. The \nmost dangerous heat will be focused from the Red Hills eastward,\nincluding Clark, Comanche and Barber counties, where a heat \nadvisory remains in effect Tuesday afternoon for heat indices of\n105-110. After coordination with WFO AMA, added Meade county to\nthe heat advisory this afternoon, where heat indices over 105\nare expected to be common. \n\nSeveral reliable CAM solutions suggest isolated/minimal\nconvective development near the frontal boundary at peak heating\n(4-7 pm) Tuesday afternoon. If any updrafts can survive the \nstrongly subsident ridge environment, some brief thunderstorms \nwith outflow winds/dry lightning are possible with an \ninverted-v profile. The chances of measurable rain at any one \nlocation are 15% or less, and added minimal pops in the grids. \n\nModels maintain continuity sliding the ridge axis southward to\nnear the Red River Wednesday afternoon. This and a net reduction\nof about 3C at 850 mb will allow the heat to relax back to the\n90s Wednesday, with most locations remaining less than 100. A\nheat advisory is not expected to be required Wednesday. Daylight\nWednesday will remain dry, but limited showers and\nthunderstorms may skirt the northern zones Wednesday night. \nNBM pops are correctly limited, with most activity north of \nSW KS further from the ridge's subsidence. Most locations will\nremain dry. \n\nPerhaps a better chance for scattered thunderstorms Thursday\nnight, as the strong high pressure ridge remains centered over\nWest Texas. Models project a notable shortwave on the northern\nperiphery of the ridge entering Kansas Thursday night, with some\nsignal for MCS generation at least across northern Kansas. \nNBM has increased pop grids into the likely category favoring\nthe northwest zones Thursday evening, and this is the next best\nchance of measurable rain, at least north of Dodge City. \n\nThe upper high is progged by the global models to amplify\nsignificantly this weekend, with heights nearing 600 dm near \nand west of SW KS. The afternoon heat will rebound accordingly,\nabove 100 all zones certainly by Sunday afternoon. GFS/MEX\nguidance appears too hot for this time frame, with NBM max\ntemperatures of 100-105 Sunday more reasonable.",
